---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const NOT_FOUND = {
  code: '404',
  icon: '🔍',
  title: "This page doesn't exist",
  description: 'PO-99999 was not found — check the link, or it may have been moved.',
  primary: 'Go to dashboard',
  secondary: 'Search purchase orders',
};
const FORBIDDEN = {
  code: '403',
  icon: '🔒',
  title: "You don't have access to this page",
  description: "Cost-centre approvals for CC-9001 aren't in your role. Ask your manager to add it, or open a different cost centre.",
  primary: 'Go to dashboard',
  secondary: 'Request access',
};
const SERVER_ERROR = {
  code: '500',
  icon: '⚠',
  title: 'Something went wrong on our end',
  description: 'The page failed to load. Nothing here is your fault — try again, or come back shortly.',
  primary: 'Retry',
  secondary: 'Go to dashboard',
};
---
<Gallery title="Pattern: error pages" description="The screens behind a stale bookmark, a moved record, a permission that changed, or a request that failed server-side.">
  <p class="demo-note">
    <strong>Who uses it</strong>: every user, eventually — a stale bookmark,
    a link shared before a record moved, a permission that changed since
    yesterday, a request that failed server-side. <strong>How often</strong>:
    rare per user, universal per app — every ERP hits all three of 404, 403
    and 500 sooner or later. <strong>What "done" looks like</strong>: the
    reader is never at a dead end — one clear sentence for what happened,
    and at least one real place to go next. Three status codes, ONE shape:
    icon, title, description, actions — <code>.bo-state</code> and
    <code>.bo-state--error</code>, already shipped, nothing new.
    <strong>Not for a section failing to load inside a page you're still
    on</strong> — a table or panel that can't fetch its own data is
    <a href={base + '/components/state-patterns'}><code>.bo-state--error</code>
    used inline</a>, right where the section was, with the rest of the
    screen still usable. This pattern is for when the NAVIGATION itself
    failed, not one region of a page that otherwise worked.
  </p>

  <section class="demo" style="background: var(--bo-color-bg-canvas); padding: var(--bo-space-8) var(--bo-space-4)">
    <div class="bo-state bo-state--error" style="max-inline-size: 28rem; margin-inline: auto">
      <span class="bo-state__icon" aria-hidden="true">{NOT_FOUND.icon}</span>
      <p class="bo-state__title">{NOT_FOUND.title}</p>
      <p class="bo-state__description">{NOT_FOUND.description}</p>
      <div class="bo-state__actions">
        <a class="bo-btn" href="#">{NOT_FOUND.primary}</a>
        <a class="bo-btn bo-btn--secondary" href="#">{NOT_FOUND.secondary}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Three codes, one shape</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Same structure every time — only the icon,
    the sentence, and which actions make sense change. A reader who has
    seen one of these has seen all three.</p>
    <div class="bo-grid">
      {[FORBIDDEN, SERVER_ERROR].map((e) => (
        <div class="bo-state bo-state--error">
          <span class="bo-state__icon" aria-hidden="true">{e.icon}</span>
          <p class="bo-state__title">{e.title}</p>
          <p class="bo-state__description">{e.description}</p>
          <div class="bo-state__actions">
            <a class="bo-btn" href="#">{e.primary}</a>
            <a class="bo-btn bo-btn--secondary" href="#">{e.secondary}</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>The description always names the
    specific thing</strong> — "PO-99999", "CC-9001" — never a bare "Not
    found" or "Access denied". Same bar the field-error and validation-
    summary patterns already hold: name the datum, not just the category.
    <strong>The action set is the one real difference between codes</strong>:
    404 and 403 offer navigation, never a retry — the resource genuinely
    isn't there or genuinely isn't yours, and a retry button that can't
    possibly help is a false promise. 500 is the one case retry can
    actually fix, so it's the primary action there instead.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Markup</h2>
    <pre><code>{`<div class="bo-state bo-state--error">
  <span class="bo-state__icon" aria-hidden="true">🔍</span>
  <p class="bo-state__title">This page doesn't exist</p>
  <p class="bo-state__description">PO-99999 was not found — check the
    link, or it may have been moved.</p>
  <div class="bo-state__actions">
    <a class="bo-btn" href="/">Go to dashboard</a>
    <a class="bo-btn bo-btn--secondary" href="/pos">Search purchase orders</a>
  </div>
</div>`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Anatomy</h2>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Canvas, no persistent chrome</strong> — like
      <a href={base + '/patterns/login'}>login</a>, this page does not
      assume the rest of the app is reachable: a 403 in particular may
      mean the sidebar's own links would also fail. Centered on
      <code>--bo-color-bg-canvas</code>, same as any screen outside normal
      navigation.</li>
      <li><strong>The state block</strong> — <a href={base + '/components/state-patterns'}><code>.bo-state
      bo-state--error</code></a>, unchanged from the fragment-level
      primitive. Full navigation and inline-swap failure share one
      component on purpose — see States below.</li>
      <li><strong>Icon</strong> — <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>; the
      title and description carry the accessible signal, never the glyph
      alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Title + description</strong> — what happened, in one
      sentence each, naming the specific record or resource where one
      exists.</li>
      <li><strong>Actions</strong> — one or two <a href={base + '/components/button'}>buttons</a>,
      chosen per code (see above); never zero. A dead end is the one
      failure mode this pattern exists to prevent.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Data contract</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Request</th><th scope="col">Returns</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">GET /pos/:id (missing)</td><td>a <strong>real 404</strong>, full HTML page — never a 200 with "not found" text; a search engine, a monitoring probe and a browser's own error handling all key off the status line, not the copy</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">GET /pos/:id (no access)</td><td>a <strong>real 403</strong>, same page shape, the permission-specific copy</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">any request, server fault</td><td>a <strong>real 500</strong>; the copy never guesses at a cause the server doesn't actually know</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">an htmx fragment request that 404s/403s/500s</td><td>the SAME <code>.bo-state--error</code> markup, sized for the swap target rather than the whole viewport — the shape doesn't change, only its container does. htmx 4 swaps non-2xx responses by default; exclude a status explicitly via <code>htmx.config.noSwap</code> if the swap target should NOT show it</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>States</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">What ships</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Full navigation (404/403/500 at the URL)</td><td>this page's own shape, canvas-centered, real HTTP status</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Inside a fragment swap</td><td>the identical <code>.bo-state--error</code> markup sized to the swap target — a table that fails to load shows this INSIDE itself, the rest of the screen stays usable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>500, retryable</td><td>Retry is the primary action; a repeat failure shows the same page again rather than a different, more alarming one — consistency over escalation</td></tr>
          <tr><td>404 / 403, not retryable</td><td>no Retry button at all — offering one for a resource that is genuinely gone or genuinely not yours is a false promise, not politeness</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Not authenticated, vs. authenticated-but-forbidden</td><td>two different things a 403 must never conflate: a signed-out visitor gets redirected to <a href={base + '/patterns/login'}>login</a>, never this page; THIS page is only for a signed-in user whose role genuinely lacks the permission</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No JS</td><td>fully functional — every element here is a plain link or the browser's own navigation; nothing on this page depends on JS running at all</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Components used</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><span class="bo-badge">complexity 1 of 4</span></p>
    <ul class="bo-cluster docs-list-bare">
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/state-patterns'}>State</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/button'}>Button</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ['/components/state-patterns', 'Loading, empty & error states'],
    ['/patterns/login', 'Login'],
    ['/concepts/accessibility', 'Accessibility model'],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
